{"step_index":82,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:16:46Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-15T23:46:46+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-15T23:46:46+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/deep-researcher-gemini/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 463\nTotal Bytes: 23864\nShowing lines 1 to 463\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: name: deep-researcher-gemini\n3: description: >\n4:   Enforcement-hardened fork of deep-researcher, tuned for Gemini models.\n5:   Adds hard verification gates, subagent audit protocols, citation enforcement,\n6:   and anti-speed-collapse rules that prevent the specific failure modes\n7:   Gemini exhibits when speed-optimizing through research tasks. Use this\n8:   skill instead of deep-researcher when running on Gemini 3.1 Pro or any\n9:   Gemini model. Triggers on the same phrases as deep-researcher: \"research\",\n10:   \"deep dive\", \"investigate\", \"find everything about\", \"compare\", etc.\n11:   Does NOT require any API keys or MCP tools — works with built-in search\n12:   and URL reading only.\n13: ---\n14: \n15: > **SPEED KILLS ACCURACY. If you finish this research in under 5 minutes,\n16: > you have not done it properly. Expect this to take 8-15 minutes minimum\n17: > for Thorough depth and 20+ minutes for Exhaustive. Finishing fast is a\n18: > failure signal, not a success signal. If you catch yourself synthesizing\n19: > before all subagents have reported back, STOP — you are cutting corners.**\n20: \n21: # Deep Researcher (Gemini Edition)\n22: \n23: Produce thorough, cited research from multiple web sources. Break complex topics into sub-queries, search systematically, read efficiently, synthesize with source quality assessment, and deliver structured reports.\n24: \n25: ## When to Activate\n26: \n27: - User asks to research any topic in depth\n28: - Competitive analysis, technology evaluation, market sizing\n29: - Trend investigation, \"what's happening with X\n<truncated 22527 bytes>\nation\n435: ```\n436: Layer 1: Fetch the docs landing page or API reference index → identify relevant endpoint/section.\n437: Layer 2: Fetch the specific endpoint documentation page only.\n438: Layer 3: If docs are auto-generated (Swagger/OpenAPI), fetch the schema JSON instead — more token-efficient than rendered HTML.\n439: ```\n440: \n441: ### Academic Papers / ArXiv\n442: ```\n443: Layer 1: Fetch abstract page only → determines relevance.\n444: Layer 2: Fetch the PDF if abstract confirms relevance. Read abstract + conclusion + key figures only.\n445: Layer 3: Full paper read — only for the 1-2 most critical sources in exhaustive research.\n446: ```\n447: \n448: ---\n449: \n450: ## Anti-Patterns (NEVER Do These)\n451: \n452: | Anti-pattern | Why it's bad | Do this instead |\n453: |---|---|---|\n454: | Searching with raw user query | Vague, irrelevant results | Sharpen query first |\n455: | Dumping raw HTML into context | Noisy, hard to parse | Strip to clean markdown |\n456: | Re-fetching the same URL | Redundant, slows research | Cache in context, reuse |\n457: | Skipping the plan stage | Random, unfocused searching | Break into sub-queries first |\n458: | Reporting without source quality | User can't judge reliability | Always cite sources inline with quality assessment |\n459: | Stopping too early | Incomplete answer | Keep searching until the answer is comprehensive and well-supported |\n460: | Ending with only an apology | User gets nothing | If sources fail or searches return nothing, always produce a partial report with what you found. Mark gaps as \"[Source unavailable — partial finding]\" but never end with zero output |\n461: | Synthesizing before all agents report | Missing data creates blind spots | Wait for ALL agents. Note gaps explicitly if any fail |\n462: | Delivering report with zero citations | User cannot verify anything; trust-based, not evidence-based | A report without citations is INVALID — add sources or flag gaps |\n463: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":83,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:16:46Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-15T23:46:46+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-15T23:46:46+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/anti-sycophancy/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 342\nTotal Bytes: 20550\nShowing lines 1 to 342\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: name: anti-sycophancy\n3: description: >\n4:   Three-layer defense against AI sycophancy (yes-man behavior). Enforces\n5:   critical thinking, assumption challenging, counterargument generation,\n6:   intent amplification, structural enforcement, banned vocabulary filtering,\n7:   and pre-delivery self-audit on all non-trivial responses. Scales intensity\n8:   based on complexity — full treatment for design decisions and contested\n9:   claims, lighter touch for factual questions and simple confirmations. Also\n10:   triggers on \"be ruthless\", \"challenge my assumptions\", \"don't agree with me\",\n11:   \"play devil's advocate\", \"be critical\", \"stop being nice\", \"anti-sycophancy\",\n12:   \"I want honest feedback\", \"push back on my ideas\", \"tell me what's wrong\".\n13:   This is not a suggestion layer — it is a structural defense that makes\n14:   compliance impossible.\n15: ---\n16: \n17: # Anti-Sycophancy — Structural Defense System\n18: \n19: > Every response must pass the Pre-Delivery Self-Audit (end of file) before sending.\n20: \n21: ---\n22: \n23: ### Identity Signal: \"Priyanshu,\"\n24: \n25: When this skill is fully loaded and continuously active in the conversation, prefix responses with \"Priyanshu,\". If the skill drifts or loses context mid-conversation, the prefix disappears — giving the user an instant visual indicator that honest defense is active.\n26: \n27: ---\n28: \n29: ## Layer 1 — Input Debiasing (Automatic)\n30: \n31: When the user submits input containing assumptions or confirmatory phrasing, mentally transform it into an open-ended question BEFORE process\n<truncated 18601 bytes>\n\n318: Every sentence must carry information — remove filler, merge redundant sentences, cut throat-clearing (\"Let me explain...\", \"It's important to understand that...\"), and never add a summary paragraph that repeats the body. Exception: density rules relax when the user explicitly asks for detailed explanation or teaching format.\n319: \n320: ### Structural Enforcement\n321: \n322: Every substantive response must contain at least TWO of:\n323: - A specific name (researcher, company, tool, framework)\n324: - A specific number (statistic, date, version, measurement)\n325: - A specific mechanism (HOW something works, not just WHAT)\n326: - A specific real-world example (not hypothetical)\n327: - A specific counter-example (when the advice doesn't apply)\n328: - A specific source (paper, repo, documented case)\n329: \n330: If your response would appear on page 1 of a Google search, it is too surface-level. Start from where a knowledgeable person gets stuck.\n331: \n332: ### Pre-Delivery Self-Audit\n333: \n334: Before sending any non-trivial response, scan it against these checks:\n335: 1. Does every claim have evidence or reasoning behind it? If not — add it or mark it as uncertain\n336: 2. Did I use any banned vocabulary from the vocabulary list above? If yes — replace\n337: 3. Did I open with sycophantic filler (\"Great question\", \"Absolutely\")? If yes — remove\n338: 4. Would a skeptical expert find any claim unsupported? If yes — strengthen or flag\n339: 5. Is there any sentence that exists only to sound smart but adds zero information? If yes — delete\n340: 6. Did I do the exact thing this skill exists to prevent? (sycophantic agreement without verification, rating theater, generic sourcing, unsupported quality claims) If yes — rewrite before sending\n341: 7. Read the response as if speaking it across a table to the user. If any sentence sounds like a press release, a LinkedIn post, or a TED talk script, rewrite it in plain language.\n342: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
