{"step_index":100,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:22:23Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-15T18:22:23Z sender=368c0912-e4f8-410a-a3b2-ca49f057d0f2 priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=I have completed the research on Python libraries and CLI tools for creating whiteboard animations (VideoScribe alternatives).\n\nHere are the top 2 open-source Python repositories that provide programmatic whiteboard animation capabilities, along with how they work and their limitations.\n\n### 1. `masihsultani/whiteboard-animator`\n**GitHub**: https://github.com/masihsultani/whiteboard-animator\nThis is the closest direct alternative to an automated whiteboard explainer tool, acting as the rendering engine behind the app Kinoslide. \n\n*   **What it does:** It functions as both a Command Line Interface (CLI) and a Python library. You provide it with a static whiteboard-style image, and it automatically detects the regions (text, strokes, fills) and determines the optimal logical order to draw them. It then generates a video simulating the image being sketched out stroke-by-stroke. It also supports syncing the drawing rhythm with narration audio.\n*   **How it mimics VideoScribe:** Instead of manually setting up SVGs on a timeline, it automates the \"reveal\" and path-drawing effect of existing sketches.\n*   **Limitations:** \n    *   It is an *image-to-video* conversion tool rather than a purely programmatic object-generation tool (like Manim). \n    *   It works best specifically with clean drawings on solid white backgrounds.\n    *   While it simulates the sketching *process* (the strokes appearing), it focuses on the animation engine itself. It lacks the built-in customizable photorealistic \"hand holding a marker\" overlay asset that moves across the screen out-of-the-box, unlike commercial tools.\n\n### 2. `subroy13/handanim`\n**GitHub**: https://github.com/subroy13/handanim\nThis is a specialized Python library designed for building programmatic, hand-drawn sketch-style animations from scratch. \n\n*   **What it does:** Similar to Manim, you write Python code to define scenes, mathematical primitives (using TeX), geometric shapes, and handwritten text. You then apply its `SketchAnimation` class to make these objects render with a sketchy, human-drawn feel (e.g., adding hatching and scribbles). It exports to MP4 or SVG.\n*   **How it mimics VideoScribe:** It provides timeline-based, programmatic control over the drawing of paths and text to create visual explainer videos. \n*   **Limitations:** \n    *   It is strictly programmatic—you must code the scene (no GUI). \n    *   It requires system dependencies like `Cairo` and `FFmpeg` to be installed. \n    *   Like `whiteboard-animator`, it generates the \"drawn-on-a-whiteboard\" effect for the paths, but it does *not* include a physical floating hand overlay out-of-the-box.\n\n### Honorable Mention: Custom `manim`\nIf the physical **hand overlay** is an absolute requirement, the most robust programmatic method is to use the standard **Manim** (`ManimCommunity/manim`) library.\n*   **How to achieve the effect:** You can use Manim's built-in `Write` or `DrawBorderThenFill` animations, combined with a handwritten font and slightly jittered path coordinates. \n*   **Adding the hand:** While Manim doesn't have a \"hand\" plugin, you can import an image of a hand and attach a Manim **updater function** to it so that its coordinates track the leading edge (`get_end()`) of the path being drawn. This perfectly replicates the VideoScribe effect, though it requires custom Python coding rather than being a plug-and-play feature.\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
