{"step_index":104,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:22:44Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-15T18:22:44Z sender=5833c1d2-eb1d-4ebc-a6df-d59731e1787b priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=I have researched open-source JavaScript, React, and Remotion repositories for VideoScribe-style whiteboard animations where a hand PNG draws an SVG path.\n\nHere are the top 2 repositories that provide complete, out-of-the-box solutions, along with their mechanics and limitations:\n\n### 1. NadirWeb-App/Inkplainer-OS\n**Overview:** A free, open-source whiteboard animation maker that runs entirely in the browser using pure JavaScript and HTML5.\n**How it works:** It acts as a full VideoScribe alternative GUI. It processes SVGs and text, animating them stroke-by-stroke. It includes a \"Drawing Mode\" that gives users control over hand behavior, direction, and stroke timing. Everything is processed locally via `IndexedDB` and it exports directly to MP4/WebM.\n**Limitations:** \n- It is a standalone GUI web app, not an importable React component or Remotion template that you can just `npm install`. \n- No backend support or programmable API; it relies on the user interacting with the browser GUI.\n\n### 2. Vincentwei1021/anything2explainer\n**Overview:** An open-source tool and \"agent skill\" built on React and **Remotion**. It takes a topic and programmatically renders a motion-graphics explainer video.\n**How it works:** Instead of relying on generative AI video, it uses Remotion to programmatically draw every frame on a \"black canvas,\" complete with vector line art, progress bars, and TTS voiceovers. It orchestrates the entire pipeline from storyboard to parallel rendering of shots.\n**Limitations:** \n- It's heavily opinionated (black canvas, specific typography) and built as an agent tool (for Claude Code/Codex) rather than a lightweight whiteboard animation library.\n- It uses a **PolyForm Noncommercial license**, meaning it cannot be used for commercial projects.\n\n### How Developers Implement \"The Hand Tracking\" in React/Remotion \nWhile there isn't a single definitive, drop-in `@remotion/whiteboard` component library, developers typically build the effect themselves using these mechanics:\n1. **React / Framer Motion:** They animate the SVG `pathLength` property using `framer-motion`.\n2. **Remotion:** Using the `@remotion/paths` package, developers use the `getPointAtLength()` function. They animate the path's progress over time, drawing the partial stroke. They then pass the exact `(x, y)` coordinates returned by `getPointAtLength()` to an absolute-positioned `<Img src=\"hand.png\" />`, ensuring the hand strictly follows the tip of the line being drawn.\n\n(A notable mention is `ytrofr/claude-remotion-editor` which includes a \"SceneDirector\" to interactively record mouse/hand paths over Remotion compositions, and `Danilaa1/drawably` which generates \"boiling\" hand-drawn UI sketch lines in React without the hand PNG).\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
{"step_index":105,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:22:44Z","content":"Priyanshu,\n\n### ANSWER\nThe sub-agents have completed their deep research across Python, React/Remotion, and Blender GitHub repositories. You are correct that open-source developers have built the mechanics to replicate VideoScribe. The two most powerful programmatic engines for this are `gnipbao/whiteboard-video-engine` (a Python CLI backend) and the Remotion `@remotion/paths` architecture. \n\n### WHY \n\nHere are the specific repos and architectures that actually work programmatically:\n\n**1. The Python CLI Engine: `gnipbao/whiteboard-video-engine`**\n*   **What it does:** This is a dedicated backend rendering engine originally built to pair with OpenAI Codex. It lacks a GUI entirely.\n*   **How it works:** You feed it SVGs, images, or scripts via the CLI. It uses 30 built-in visual \"recipes\" that dictate stroke textures, line widths, and fill behaviors, then handles the rendering and composition using FFmpeg under the hood. \n\n**2. The Remotion Explainer: `Vincentwei1021/anything2explainer`**\n*   **What it does:** An open-source tool built on React and Remotion that programmatically renders motion-graphics explainer videos with vector line art and TTS voiceovers.\n*   **How it tracks the hand (The standard Remotion architecture):** Even without a full repo, the open-source community achieves the VideoScribe effect in Remotion using a specific mathematical trick. By animating an SVG's `stroke-dashoffset`, you reveal the line. Simultaneously, you pass the path to `@remotion/paths` and use the `getPointAtLength()` function. This outputs the exact `(X, Y)` coordinates of the tip of the line on every frame. You then absolute-position your `<Img src=\"hand_holding_marker.png\" />` to those coordinates, forcing the hand to perfectly track the drawing line.\n\n**3. The Image-to-Video CLI: `masihsultani/whiteboard-animator`**\n*   **What it does:** A Python CLI tool that takes a static whiteboard-style image, detects the regions (text, strokes, fills), determines the logical drawing order, and generates a video simulating the sketch\n<truncated 26 bytes>\ns the drawing rhythm with audio. (Note: It focuses on the drawing reveal, not the physical hand overlay).\n\n### COUNTER\n\nYou assume that because the open-source code exists, you can just plug it in and it will work smoothly like VideoScribe. This is a false assumption.\n\nOpen-source developers write excellent math to trace vector lines, but they do NOT have the budget to create the 10,000+ proprietary SVGs that VideoScribe provides. VideoScribe works perfectly because human artists manually programmed the *draw-path order* into every single asset. \n\nIf you use `whiteboard-video-engine` or Remotion and feed it a standard free SVG from the internet, the computer will just mathematically trace the raw vector coordinates. It might draw the roof of a house, then jump to a window, then jump to the grass, then back to the door. The \"hand\" tracking those coordinates will jerk erratically across the screen, looking like a robot glitching out rather than a human drawing smoothly.\n\n### CONDITIONS\n\nBuilding your own CLI automation using these GitHub repos is only viable if you are willing to open your SVGs in Adobe Illustrator or Inkscape and manually reorganize the layer/path order for every single graphic before feeding it to your script. \n\nIf you just want to download a script, type a command, and have it magically turn random images into beautiful human-drawn whiteboard animations without you touching the vector paths, no open-source repository can do that. That level of out-of-the-box perfection only exists in paid, closed-ecosystem tools.\n\n### SOURCES\n*   **Python Engine:** [github.com/gnipbao/whiteboard-video-engine](https://github.com/gnipbao/whiteboard-video-engine)\n*   **Image-to-Video CLI:** [github.com/masihsultani/whiteboard-animator](https://github.com/masihsultani/whiteboard-animator)\n*   **Remotion AI Explainer:** [github.com/Vincentwei1021/anything2explainer](https://github.com/Vincentwei1021/anything2explainer) (Note: PolyForm Noncommercial license)\n*   **Remotion API:** `@remotion/paths` (`getPointAtLength`)","thinking":"**Synthesizing Open-Source Options**\n\nI've received the reports from all three sub-agents and am now focusing on synthesizing the best open-source alternatives for the user. My emphasis is on finding solutions that closely mimic VideoScribe, particularly those offering terminal/CLI or programmatic generation capabilities.\n\n\n**Organizing Engine Findings**\n\nI've organized the Python engines into a cohesive group, highlighting their CLI-driven, backend-oriented strengths. I'm focusing on `gnipbao/whiteboard-video-engine`, `masihsultani/whiteboard-animator`, and `subroy13/handanim` to categorize their distinct functionalities. I'm also now analyzing the Remotion and React-based solutions, and specifically the Remotion hand-tracking builds.\n\n\n**Evaluating UI & Art Libraries**\n\nI've explored options for the user. I've found a Remotion solution that can mimic VideoScribe perfectly by mapping coordinates to an image. Also, I've identified `NadirWeb-App/Inkplainer-OS` as a browser-based VideoScribe clone, but it's a UI tool, not a CLI tool. Finally, `doakey3/GreaseWriter`, a Blender add-on for hand-drawn text animations, could be useful. I'm focusing on the art library issue now.\n\n\n**Analyzing Asset Dependencies**\n\nI'm now focusing on a critical asset-related catch. I've pinpointed `gnipbao/whiteboard-video-engine` as a strong CLI engine but it requires SVGs or scripts for input. I'm also now thinking more deeply about customizing Remotion to solve the art dependency. Remotion uses `@remotion/paths` to find the exact (X,Y) coordinates of the drawing line at any frame, in effect.\n\n\n**Addressing Asset Constraints**\n\nI'm now grappling with asset dependency. Specifically, with `gnipbao/whiteboard-video-engine`, I understand that while mathematically precise, any SVG will look bad without a well-ordered drawing path. I've been thinking about adapting Remotion for precise positioning, and an `<Img src=\"hand.png\" />`, and using a Grease Pencil add-on in Blender. I see the potential for `doakey3/GreaseWriter` to add a bit of the human touch. The brutal truth is I will have to create new assets.\n\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
