{"step_index":107,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:24:26Z","content":"Priyanshu,\n\n### ANSWER\nHere is the master list of every tool we have uncovered in our research, categorized by exactly what they do. \n\nTo help you out based on your specific goals, I have separated them into two distinct tech stacks: the **Nitish Rajput Geopolitics Stack** (cinematic graphics) and the **Atomic Habits Whiteboard Stack** (vector sketching), along with the open-source and AI tools that power them.\n\n### WHY\n\n#### 1. The \"Nitish Rajput\" Style Stack (Cinematic & Geopolitics)\n*   **Adobe After Effects:** The heavy-duty animation software used to create the smooth, cinematic motion graphics, parallax photo effects, and moving text seen in Nitish Rajput's videos.\n*   **GeoLayers 3 (After Effects Plugin):** The specific plugin used to generate the high-quality, zooming 3D maps that are mandatory for geopolitical channels.\n*   **Adobe Premiere Pro / DaVinci Resolve:** Timeline editors used to cut the final video together, add sound effects, and splice in B-roll footage. DaVinci is the free alternative.\n\n#### 2. The \"Atomic Habits\" Style Stack (Commercial Whiteboard GUIs)\n*   **VideoScribe:** The exact paid software used to create the Atomic Habits video you had me analyze. It auto-animates a human hand drawing SVG vectors on a white canvas.\n*   **Doodly:** A budget-friendly, one-time-purchase alternative to VideoScribe with slightly fewer features but the exact same hand-drawing aesthetic.\n*   **Explaindio:** Another commercial GUI software that mixes whiteboard sketching with standard 2D animation.\n\n#### 3. The Open-Source / CLI Automated Stack (GitHub Alternatives)\n*   **Remotion:** A React-based framework that lets you generate video using code. By using the `@remotion/paths` library, you can programmatically calculate SVG coordinates and attach a PNG hand to automate the VideoScribe effect via CLI.\n*   **`whiteboard-video-engine` (Python):** A headless CLI engine that takes SVGs and text via terminal commands and renders them into whiteboard animations using FFmpeg.\n*   **`whiteboard-animator` (Python):\n<truncated 1 bytes>\n* A CLI tool that takes a static image (like a chart or drawing) and converts it into a video showing that image being drawn stroke-by-stroke, synced to audio.\n*   **`handanim` (Python):** A coding library for building mathematical or educational sketches from scratch, outputting video with sketchy, human-drawn fills.\n*   **`Inkplainer-OS` (Web):** A completely free, open-source browser-based clone of VideoScribe (features a GUI, not CLI).\n*   **`GreaseWriter` (Blender):** A Blender Python add-on that physically simulates human writing physics for text, complete with organic pauses between pen strokes.\n\n#### 4. The AI Automation Layer (For your Faceless Workflow)\n*   **ElevenLabs:** The absolute best text-to-speech AI for generating hyper-realistic English and Hindi voiceovers.\n*   **Suno:** Generates custom background music based on text prompts to lay underneath your narration.\n*   **Envato Elements / Freepik:** Massive libraries where you download the raw SVG graphics, photos, and B-roll to feed into your animation tools.\n\n### COUNTER\n\nYou asked me to help you based on *both* the Nitish Rajput channel AND the whiteboard video you had me analyze. The harsh truth is that these two references represent fundamentally opposing production styles. \n\nYou cannot build a Nitish Rajput-level geopolitical channel using open-source whiteboard scripts or VideoScribe. Geopolitics requires maps, archival footage, and serious cinematic tones. Conversely, you do not use After Effects and GeoLayers to summarize *Atomic Habits* with stickmen. \n\n### CONDITIONS\n\nBefore you pick a tool to learn or automate, you must make the final decision on your niche. \n*   **If you choose Geopolitics:** You must abandon the whiteboard automation idea and commit to