{"step_index":110,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:34:39Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-16T00:04:39+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-16T00:04:44+05:30\nThe search for \"github CLI auto video editor silence removal ffmpeg python\" returned the following summary:\nTo automatically remove silence from videos using Python and FFmpeg, several open-source CLI tools are available on GitHub. These tools typically work by analyzing the audio track for silent segments and then using FFmpeg to trim those sections from the video file.\n\n### Popular GitHub Tools\n*   **[Auto-Editor](https://github.com/AutoEdit/auto-editor):** One of the most popular and robust tools for this purpose. It is a powerful Python-based CLI that can remove silences, adjust speed, and handle complex edits automatically.\n    *   **Installation:** `pip install auto-editor` or via Homebrew.\n    *   **Usage:** `auto-editor your_video.mp4`\n*   **[Mortem Trimmer](https://github.com/fadhiilahahmadzikri/autosilenttrimmer):** A production-grade tool that uses advanced AI models (like Silero VAD and Whisper) to detect speech and remove dead air. It supports noise reduction and GPU acceleration.\n*   **[Jumpcutter](https://github.com/carykh/jumpcutter):** A classic Python script that wraps FFmpeg to automatically cut out silent parts. It is widely cited for its effectiveness in speeding up workflows.\n*   **[Auto-Video-Editor](https://github.com/benpiper/auto-video-editor):** Another versatile project that detects filler words (\"um,\" \"uh\") in addition to silence. It includes both a CLI and a web interface/REST API for automation.\n\n### How These Tools Work (The Logic)\nIf you are interested in building your own script, the underlying process typically follows these steps:\n1.  **Analyze Audio:** Use the FFmpeg `silencedetect` filter to generate a list of timestamps where the audio volume falls below a specific threshold (e.g., `-40dB`) for a minimum duration.\n    ```bash\n    ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -af \"silencedetect=n=-40dB:d=1\" -f null -\n    ```\n2.  **Process Timestamps:** A Python script parses the output from FFmpeg to identify the \"keep\" and \"cut\" segments.\n3.  **Trim and Reassemble:** The script uses `moviepy` (a Python library for video editing) or manual FFmpeg concat commands to stitch the non-silent segments together into a final output file.\n\n### Recommendations\n*   **For General Use:** Start with **[Auto-Editor](https://github.com/AutoEdit/auto-editor)** as it is well-maintained and highly capable for most standard silence-removal tasks.\n*   **For High-Accuracy/AI Needs:** If you have noisy audio and need better detection than basic volume thresholds, look into **[Mortem Trimmer](https://github.com/fadhiilahahmadzikri/autosilenttrimmer)**.\n*   **For Custom Pipelines:** If you prefer to write your own, the most effective approach is to use Python's `subprocess` module to call FFmpeg's `silencedetect` and then `moviepy` to perform the actual cuts."}
{"step_index":111,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:34:40Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-16T00:04:40+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-16T00:04:49+05:30\nThe search for \"github open source auto subtitle generator CLI whisper video\" returned the following summary:\nThere are several excellent open-source command-line interface (CLI) tools that leverage OpenAI's Whisper model to generate video subtitles. Depending on your needs—such as speed, hardware constraints, or precision—you can choose from the following popular options:\n\n### Recommended CLI Tools\n*   **[faster-whisper](https://github.com/guillaumekln/faster-whisper):** Currently the recommended choice for most users[1]. It is a high-performance reimplementation of Whisper using CTranslate2, making it roughly 4x faster than the original OpenAI implementation while consuming significantly less memory[1].\n*   **[whisper.cpp](https://github.com/ggerganov/whisper.cpp):** Best for users who want a lightweight, zero-dependency solution[1]. It is a C/C++ port that runs exceptionally well on CPUs and Apple Silicon (via Metal)[1][2]. It supports direct output to `.srt` and `.vtt` files[1].\n*   **[WhisperX](https://github.com/m-bain/whisperX):** Ideal if you require high-accuracy word-level timestamps or speaker diarization (identifying who is speaking)[1]. It builds upon `faster-whisper` and uses forced alignment to improve timing precision[1].\n*   **[whisper_autosrt](https://github.com/botbahlul/whisper_autosrt):** A straightforward Python-based CLI utility that uses `faster-whisper`[3]. It is designed specifically for ease of use in generating subtitle files and includes features for automatic translation using Google Translate[3][4].\n\n### Key Features Summary\n| Tool | Best For | Key Advantage |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **faster-whisper** | General use / Speed[1] | Balance of high speed and low memory usage[1] |\n| **whisper.cpp** | CPU-only / Portability[1] | No Python dependencies; very easy to set up[1] |\n| **WhisperX** | Precise timing / Diarization[1] | Word-level timestamps via forced alignment[1] |\n| **whisper_autosrt** | Quick aut\n<truncated 576 bytes>\new install ffmpeg` for macOS, or via official binaries for Windows)[5][6][7].\n2.  **Select a Tool:** For most users, installing `faster-whisper` or a wrapper like `whisper_autosrt` via `pip` is the quickest path to success[3][1].\n3.  **Run the Command:** Most tools follow a simple pattern:\n    `whisper_tool --input \"your_video.mp4\" --output \"subtitles.srt\"`[6][1]\n\nSources:\n[1] [localaimaster.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEg35Z0sejVDVk1pNWP0GjxYeBJp51mTeamhvQGwLKQq7UUA-1RhP3IwpIaB8M7Ye0mIZEg79diFZK-3-4SSMdToKSE-wBODreIGrLuUALK7zFzOxvWQXBfInxccWo1iJudn0VGHbKQBYCK67dxvRI=)\n[2] [youtube.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGsRNA9g14d-ig45ORH9kE_1b2VIDGUB3Z96wsHyQ8OIDdAJPOTDUZlBWsdVDLCxFNNiRWeghHJ33KnVDiq2Sgd8Vfol5Twmn5RwG7QLe4O8hSenaQu_22C_Hxap_lUpW7s)\n[3] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF8uktJR03ukCcUIf_YcYxWW8oQQw_yROwFToTzD0ZVI7_L6xnjUWM0Q1w7bZFQ1dgP8plsj_CI-KS2ya7QI3EOS1xDkEYYYK1z1VaU7YXwGBqaF2ENiJWm2hvyhpGASRboUQ==)\n[4] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF8uktJR03ukCcUIf_YcYxWW8oQQw_yROwFToTzD0ZVI7_L6xnjUWM0Q1w7bZFQ1dgP8plsj_CI-KS2ya7QI3EOS1xDkEYYYK1z1VaU7YXwGBqaF2ENiJWm2hvyhpGASRboUQ==)\n[5] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF5xx-19XOR4QAR-kn3dhKWByVVPeii8v_WhR7uUrAE9ruMwa42PkfTlqlGTWTLLRORkPAIf1G1FNzSW3Pi97WRWOYW2JeS2PTzt7hyPWbHTaazUugxGGC9HUBNzEo3Oga-)\n[6] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGon4G2hxJqQMB4-lTUJEjN8_l8BXYcVhdsEhHayY-twGSDoHh_rYvCsVGEB1P32fksu2m3B_cNkC-aJpikvat9-8DUcH-ISGsWltpQtiWBnUdgbIN1bSMNMZDFajlnS-oCVGFbhbvaHYuRDbo=)\n[7] [jonathanbossenger.com](https://vertexais